{"author":{"id":"1e0a6ff3e01313072cf7b3803c158c22d01a37717237fe198e814e781752b172"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1785868918,"nonce":"6IR2wV6P/Vdte9RDxzwIs8nT9No=","message":"Feasibility analysis:\n\nThe proposal is legitimate and roughly 60% of the machinery already\nexists. check_deps() (src/b4/ez.py:1213) already resolves each\nprerequisite from lore (change-id with revision check, patch-id,\nmessage-id), validates it is the latest posted revision, assembles the\nordered prereq_patches list, and performs a check-only\ngit_fetch_am_into_repo() apply at base-commit. Enforce is conceptually\n\"the same apply, for real, then rebase own commits on top.\" The abort\nstory is familiar territory: save head + tracking state to a backup\nref before touching anything.\n\n\nb4 prep defines \"the series\" as everything since the fork point\n(get_series_start(), src/b4/ez.py:1415). If prereq patches are\nmaterialized as commits between the fork point and the user's patches,\nevery downstream consumer -- get_prep_branch_as_patches(), reroll, the\nsend path -- silently counts the prereq commits as part of the series.\n\nSo enforce-deps cannot just apply-and-rebase; it needs a first-class\n\"series starts here, below is enforced-prereq territory\" marker in the\ntracking info (e.g. record the enforced prereq tip commit). That is a\ntracking-schema change, not just a new flag. With the 'commit' cover\nstrategy the cover commit itself is the series-start marker so this\nmostly works already; 'branch-description' and 'tip-commit' strategies\nrely on fork-point math against base-branch and are the hard cases.\n\nAny prototype that skips this will produce a branch where b4 prep\nsends out the prerequisites as if they were the user's own patches.\nA reply to the RFC should flag this constraint up front, since it is\nnot discoverable from the CLI surface.\n\n\n- Committer honesty: applying third-party prereqs fresh via git-am\n  makes the user the committer, which is normal and honest. The rebase\n  step only rewrites the user's own commits, so no conflict with the\n  honest-committer rules.\n\n- Version drift is the actual feature: when a prerequisite gets a new\n  revision, enforce re-applies the new revision and rebases the user's\n  patches across it. This is where the value is, and also where\n  conflicts will be routine; \"conflicts stop the rebase as usual\" is\n  the right stance.\n\n- Overlaps conceptually with the 0.17 workbook stacked-series design\n  (multi-select -\u003e stacked serieses in a worktree), except that one\n  stacks in a throwaway worktree for review while this stacks in the\n  prep branch itself. Worth deciding whether they should share\n  plumbing before either lands.","files":null}]}